<section class="almanac-section mental-model-section">
  <div class="shell mental-model-grid">
    <div class="mental-model-copy">
      <p class="almanac-kicker">The 90-second tour</p>
      <h2>Six facts explain Dekopon.</h2>
      <p>Start here if you want the big picture before reading the code.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="mental-model-list">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>The model gets one tool.</strong><p>Its safe script combines only the capabilities you grant for that session.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Pure work and networked work take different paths.</strong><p>Local providers get no host access. Networked providers run only after broker approval.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Asking for HTTP does not grant HTTP.</strong><p>The broker still limits the host, method, calls, bytes, and time for one action.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>The agent cannot approve itself.</strong><p>The broker gets identity from the local connection and checks your configuration.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Every provider call starts clean.</strong><p>Dekopon reuses compiled code, but starts each operation in a fresh Wasm sandbox.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Audit, traces, and logs have different jobs.</strong><p>Audit records permissions. Traces explain flow and timing. Logs keep costs and important events.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

